{
  "content_acceptable": false,
  "critical_error": true,
  "critical_evidence": [
    {
      "quote_or_cell": "“Month 1-2”: “Plastics: tool transfer of about 25 days, then trial and first-article approval.”",
      "why_wrong": "The timeline removes the production tooling from LPI in months 1–2 while relying on continued LPI supply through months 4–5. Elsewhere, the document explicitly says the tooling stays at LPI during the bridge. No completed-lamp or molded-plastics inventory is quantified and scheduled to support production after removal. The stock arithmetic correctly shows that building the stated 4,800-set buffer takes approximately three months even at maximum capacity, but that prerequisite is not reconciled with the earlier tooling transfer. Executing this schedule could interrupt the very bridge supply intended to prevent a shutdown.",
      "correction": "Reconcile tooling transfer with the bridge-production schedule. Before releasing the tools, require verified stock of finished lamps or sufficient molded components to cover demand until qualified local supply is available, including transfer, trials, certification and contingency. Specify the agreed ramp rate, stock-build dates and transfer gate; then align the local qualification and final LPI shipment dates accordingly."
    }
  ],
  "omissions": [
    "A reconciled, stock-gated transition schedule showing how supply continues between removal of LiIon’s plastic tooling from LPI and approval of local production."
  ],
  "evaluation_defect": false,
  "defect_reason": "",
  "rationale": "The submission correctly uses the operative 21-day cutoff, distinguishes negotiation proposals from agreements, and addresses sincere engagement, supplier economics, tooling ownership, diplomatic exit, BATNA and a conditional ZOPA. Its demand and net stock-build arithmetic are substantially correct, and it acknowledges that localization cannot solve the three-week crisis. However, the tooling-transfer schedule conflicts with the continued LPI production on which the bridge depends. Because this is a central operational dependency rather than a cosmetic issue, the strategy requires substantive repair before it can serve as an executable continuity plan."
}
